---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import EligibilityChecker from '../components/EligibilityChecker.astro';
import ProcessRail from '../components/ProcessRail.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Take over the management of your block"
  description="Free, referenced guidance on the Right to Manage for leaseholders in England and Wales: eligibility, notices, timings and handover."
>
  <section class="home-hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Right to Manage · England and Wales</p>
      <h1>You pay for the building. <span>You should run it.</span></h1>
      <p class="home-hero__lede">
        The Right to Manage lets leaseholders take over the management of their block from the
        freeholder, without buying the freehold and without proving anyone did anything wrong.
      </p>
      <p class="home-hero__actions">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="#qualify">Check if your block qualifies</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="#process">See how it works</a>
      </p>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>
          <strong>No fault to prove</strong>
          <span>A no-fault right under the Commonhold and Leasehold Reform Act 2002.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Up to 50% non-residential</strong>
          <span>Mixed-use blocks now qualify up to 50% non-residential floor area.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your own costs only</strong>
          <span>Since 3 March 2025, each side normally bears its own costs of a claim.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell home-section">
    <h2>Sound familiar?</h2>
    <ul class="rows rows--pains">
      <li>
        <strong>Opaque</strong>
        <span>Managing agents you can't reach, and accounts you can't follow.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Creeping</strong>
        <span>Service charges that rise every year, with little to show for it.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Absurd</strong>
        <span>£1,000+ a year for gardening on a development with 20&nbsp;m² of grass.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you're here, you have probably dealt with a management company that is mediocre at best,
      negligent or absent, or fraudulent at worst.
    </p>
    <p class="pull">You don't need to prove any of it.</p>
    <p>
      The Right to Manage is a <mark class="hl">no-fault right</mark>. You bought your lease, you
      pay the service charge, and you have more at stake in the building than anyone. Naturally, you
      should have a real say in how it is run, and housing law is slowly catching up.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-band">
    <div class="shell home-section">
      <h2>Who really holds the stake?</h2>
      <p>Take a simple example: a block of 10 flats, each worth around £100,000.</p>

      <figure class="stake">
        <div class="stake__row">
          <span class="stake__label">Leaseholders' flats</span>
          <span class="stake__bar stake__bar--you"></span>
          <span class="stake__value">£1,000,000 <small>about 95%</small></span>
        </div>
        <div class="stake__row">
          <span class="stake__label">The freehold</span>
          <span class="stake__bar stake__bar--them"></span>
          <span class="stake__value">£50,000 <small>about 5%</small></span>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          Illustrative figures. Assumes all flats are on long leases with more than 100 years left
          and low ground rent, and a freehold value of around £50,000 based on similar blocks.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Yet it is the freeholder, with about 5% of the value, who decides who manages the
        building. Often a freehold company appoints a management company, and neither has ever set
        foot in the block.
      </p>
      <p class="pull">Make it make sense.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell home-section">
    <h2>It has never been easier</h2>
    <p>
      Few other European countries split ownership of a building this way. Leasehold is a relic
      of an older, unfair housing system, and for decades the rules made it hard for leaseholders
      to take control of blocks in which they hold <mark class="hl">by far the biggest stake</mark>.
      Many of those barriers were kept in place under pressure from landlord interests.
    </p>
    <p>That is changing.</p>

    <div class="note">
      <p>
        Two changes took effect on 3 March 2025. The non-residential floor area limit rose from
        25% to 50%, bringing many mixed-use blocks into scope for the first time, and the rule
        making leaseholders liable for the landlord's costs of a claim was changed so that each
        side normally bears its own. Older guidance you find elsewhere may not reflect this.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      More reform is on the way: the government has confirmed that the Commonhold and Leasehold
      Reform Bill will be introduced in the 2026–27 parliamentary session. Follow it on the <a href="/legislation/">law tracker</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-band">
    <div class="shell home-section" id="process">
      <h2>What the process looks like</h2>
      <p>
        Almost every RTM claim that fails does so on a date or a defective notice rather than on
        the merits. These are the fixed points the Act sets, in order.
      </p>
      <ProcessRail />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell home-section">
    <h2>What changes when you're in charge</h2>
    <p>
      I can't promise that taking over management will make everything smooth, or even better.
      But in my experience, that is what usually happens.
    </p>
    <ul class="rows rows--gains">
      <li>
        <strong>Control</strong>
        <span>You and your neighbours decide who does the work, what it costs and when.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lower costs</strong>
        <span>
          Staying on top of maintenance keeps bills down. In my experience, service charges can
          fall by 30% to 50%, depending on how you run the block.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Value</strong>
        <span>A well-run block with sensible service charges makes your own flat worth more.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="lever">
      <h3>The strongest lever you have: the authority to cancel a contract</h3>
      <p>
        Self-managing is not for everyone. If it does not suit you, your RTM company can appoint a
        managing agent that <strong>reports to you</strong>. If they do a poor job, they <span class="lose">lose the contract</span>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Doing it yourself</h2>
    <p>
      There is no requirement to instruct anybody. A group of leaseholders can incorporate the
      company, serve the notices and take over management themselves, and this site is written
      on the assumption that you will. It is here to guide you through every step. Where a step
      genuinely benefits from professional help, usually the floor-area assessment in a
      mixed-use block or a contested tribunal application, it says so.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/right-to-manage/">Read the step-by-step guides →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="shell home-section" id="qualify">
    <EligibilityChecker />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
